# THE ISSUE TRIAGE DRIVE: a new issue is read against the code, by whoever is at a keyboard.
#
# One half, two strata, two rules. A git-issue appears; a row is derived for it; a task is
# spawned that NAMES `broker`, so it lands `unclaimed` and waits for a person's session to claim
# it (.claude/skills/verse-work). Naming it is the whole point: silence means versed runs the
# task itself, immediately. The answer lands back as a fact on the row.
#
# It is deliberately the small mirror of comment-drift: that one asks whether a comment still
# agrees with its code and answers with a branch, this one asks whether an ISSUE still agrees
# with its code and answers with prose. Same question, different subject, and no push.
#
#     verse charter -a -f docs/working/issue-triage.tony
#
# INSTALLING BRIEFS THE BACKLOG, once, and that is the design rather than a hazard to work
# around. Every open verse issue gets a row and a task the moment this installs — 43 of them
# today — because the charter cannot tell a new issue from an old one and should not pretend to.
#
# It cannot because nothing in the grammar can say it. `Op` is `set` or `delete` and
# create-vs-update "needs prior existence the substrate does not carry" (docs/change-events.md),
# so "this entity just appeared" is unsayable; and the match operators are and/or/not/glob/
# has-path/let and their kin, with no comparison, so "created after now" is unsayable too. A
# rule over `git-issue.issue.*` selects the whole backlog, and there is no predicate that does
# otherwise.
#
# WHAT NOT TO DO ABOUT IT: latch the existing rows first, so only later ones fire. That is a
# write per row, and every one of them wakes this rule again over the rows not yet latched — a
# rule re-selects its whole set on any commit, and "a rule acting on member A writes A; that
# commit re-evaluates the rule, which selects B, already matching and never brought in by
# anything" (docs/cycle-throttle.md). So the latching IS the storm it means to prevent, and it
# spawns for whatever it has not reached yet. It fails worse the more rows there are.
#
# So the backlog is briefed, and a task nobody wants is CANCELLED — which is terminal here, and
# the reason `onCancelled` latches rather than clearing. A cancel that cleared the latch would
# put the row back in this rule's selection and spawn it again, which is a loop with a person
# at one end of it.
at: verse-dev.triage
reads: [ !verse-path [git-issue] ]
---
# The reflected slice, where an ISSUE is the subject. Level 0 because nothing this package
# writes is upstream of it: the source writes here and this package never does.
- name: reflected
  level: 0
  match:
    system: git-issue

# One row per issue, keyed by the issue. It exists so the briefing has a subject of its OWN —
# a task's id is (rule, subject), and the issue entity is written by the source on every poll,
# so a rule spawning against it directly would re-fire on `updatedAt` moving.
#
# It is also where the answer lands. The source is the SINGLE WRITER of `git-issue.*`
# (docs/sources.md: "Ownership = single writer = no CAS"), so a brief written onto the issue
# would be writing into somebody else's slice. Read theirs, write your own.
- name: brief
  level: 10
  match:
    path: [verse-dev, triage, brief]
---
# 1. An open issue in this repository gets a row.
#
# The subject is a PATH, `[git-issue, issue, "*"]`, and that is the spelling that selects. The
# same rule written as `system: git-issue` with `kind: issue` derives its rows when the charter
# is installed and never again — an issue arriving does not wake it — while rule 2 below, whose
# subject is a path, fires on every row it is given.
#
# FACTS ONLY in the value, for the reason `proposal-appeared` records: a derive MERGES its
# literal fields every run, so anything named here is restated every time the source re-reflects
# the issue. `title` is the issue's to change and is restated deliberately. `stage` is absent
# because it is state the row owns, and restating it would drag a briefed row back and spawn
# again.
- name: issue-noticed
  stratum: reflected
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [git-issue, issue, "*"]
      value:
        repo: verse
        status: open
  action:
    derive:
    - path: [verse-dev, triage, brief]
      id: $subject.value.id
      value:
        issue: $subject.value.id
        repo: $subject.value.repo
        title: $subject.value.title
---
# 2. …and a row nobody has briefed becomes a question somebody answers.
#
# `runner: broker` is the entire point of this rule. It names the dial-in broker, so the spawn
# lands `unclaimed` and waits for a person to claim it from their own session — the work happens
# where the checkout and the credentials already are, and nothing about either crosses verse.
#
# It is NAMED and not left to the default, which is what a spawn saying nothing gets: this
# verse's own runtime, running the work itself. Silence means "run this"; asking for a person is
# something a rule has to say.
#
# NO `answer:` contract either, for the same reason. A contract is what makes an LLM driver's
# final message parseable; here the executor is a person reporting through
# `verse task do complete <id> "<text>"`, which is a STRING. A shape contract would refuse every
# answer a human gives.
#
# `!not.has-path stage` is the latch, and the spelling matters: `!not {stage: null}` is what it
# looks like it should be and does not work — absence is written as a bare null and every bare
# null is hidden on read, so the durable rule comes back as `!not {}` and selects everything.
# The failure is invisible until the first restart.
- name: brief-issue
  stratum: brief
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [verse-dev, triage, brief, "*"]
      value: !not.has-path stage
  action:
    spawn:
      runner: broker
      vector: auto
      prompt: |
        A verse issue has just been filed, and nobody has read it against the code yet.

        Issue $title
        Its id is $issue, in the verse repository. `git issue show $issue` is the full text,
        labels and comments.

        ANSWER TWO QUESTIONS, in this order.

        WHAT DOES IT TOUCH. Name the files and Go symbols this issue is actually about —
        repo-relative and spelled the way Go does: `entity.JoinSegs`, `source/github.Reflect`,
        `versed.(*Server).HostBroker`. A symbol survives the code moving between files, which a
        line number does not. If the issue is about docs or deploy rather than code, name those
        paths instead and say so.

        IS IT STILL TRUE OF MAIN. Read what stands there NOW, not what the issue says stood
        there when it was filed. An issue outlives the code it was filed against: the thing may
        be fixed, may have moved, may have been made moot by something unrelated, or may be
        exactly as described. Say which, and say what you read that settles it.

        Those two together are the deliverable. "Summarise the issue" is not the task — the
        issue already says what it says, and repeating it back is a turn nobody bought.

        Read each thing once. A second read of a file does not make a verdict truer.

        WHAT TO DO WITH YOUR ANSWER: report it with

            verse task do complete <this task id> "<your answer>" --as <you>

        and narrate what you find along the way with `verse task do note`, so whoever is watching
        with `verse task attach` sees it happen. If the issue turns out to need a decision rather
        than a reading — it is ambiguous, or answering it would change something — raise a gate
        with `verse task do gate` and let somebody answer, rather than deciding it yourself.

        If you cannot settle it, that is an answer too: complete with what you established and
        what you would need to go further. `fail` is for work that could not be carried out, not
        for a question that turned out to be hard.
    onDone:
      patch:
        brief: $result
        stage: briefed
        err: !delete null
    onFailed:
      patch:
        stage: failed
        err: $err
    # A cancelled briefing LATCHES, and it is the one hook where that is load-bearing. Clearing
    # the stage would put the row back in this rule's selection, so cancelling a task would
    # spawn it again — a loop whose lap time is however long a person takes to press cancel, and
    # which the cycle throttle paces rather than stops because every lap is a real write.
    #
    # `declined` and not `failed`: nothing went wrong, somebody decided this issue does not need
    # reading. Removing the stage by hand is how it goes back on the docket.
    onCancelled:
      patch:
        stage: declined
        err: !delete null
